\name{BaseRCpgramLayerProcessor}
\alias{BaseRCpgramLayerProcessor}
\title{Base R Cumulative Periodogram Processor}
\description{
Reads \code{cpgram()} as the staircase it draws: the cumulative periodogram
against frequency, held across each interval and then jumping.

\strong{It is a step, not a line.} \code{cpgram()} plots with \code{type = "s"}, which
draws the horizontal segment first -- MAIDR's \code{"hv"}. A line would imply
the value slides between frequencies, which is not what a cumulative sum
does, and the export agrees: the grob is \code{step-1}, not \code{lines-1}.

\strong{It does NOT use \code{spectrum()}'s estimate.} This is the trap the reading
exists to avoid. \code{spectrum()} defaults to \verb{taper = 0.1, detrend = TRUE, demean = FALSE} and smooths; \code{cpgram()} computes its own periodogram --
taper, FFT, zero the first ordinate, then normalise the cumulative sum --
and the two disagree. Measured, the second step of the drawn curve is
0.0801 while \code{spectrum()}'s estimate gives 0.0817: close enough to look
right and wrong enough to announce wrong numbers. So the computation below
is \code{cpgram()}'s own, and it reproduces the traced \code{plot.xy} call exactly.
}
\keyword{internal}
